nodecode-booking is a rewrite of kyle-bartlett's kyle-bartlett/booking-bridge:

  https://github.com/kyle-bartlett/booking-bridge
  commit 8d013fcd7500f5b9e3b98588a6c32826f25c8252
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Kyle Bartlett / Bartlett Labs (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the whole of what the plugin gives a user — its five tools
(list_availability, list_upcoming, book_event, reschedule_event,
cancel_event) with their arguments and their answers, the FreeBusy check
before every write, the conflict refusal, the bookingBridgeKey idempotency
mark, the sendUpdates=all invitations, the window capped at 14 days and the
60-slot answer — as a Nodecode cell. Where the plugin called Hermes'
credentialed_provider_request for a Google token Hermes held, this cell holds
its own: a refresh grant at oauth2.googleapis.com becomes an access token in
memory, a 401 renews it once and retries, and the token never touches disk.
The plugin's /booking status command is /booking here, and its consult-booking
skill's steps (never guess slots, read the time back, confirm, then book) are
the manual behind (help :booking) and the words of the write tools' own
descriptions, since a cell's guidance reaches the model that way.

Left out: the pre_tool_call approval hook and its platform allowlist. Hermes
could ask a person to approve a write at the tool boundary; a cell has no such
boundary, so the rewrite keeps the plugin's second layer alone — confirm: true,
which the model sets only after the operator agreed — and says so in each write
tool's description and in the manual. Also left out: the IANA timezone
database. Times are read and written at a fixed UTC offset (the call's, the
section's, or this machine's zone at that instant), so a booking that crosses
a daylight-saving change carries the offset it was given rather than a zone
name; the plugin's default_timezone, default_calendar and
default_duration_minutes became the section's utc_offset, calendar and
duration_minutes.
